llow leather  enthusiast who are you under there but  the shadowy edges of gotham are brought  into sharp focus by the film's also  masked villain the riddler he repeatedly  states his grand project to shine a  light on all the city's lies he's  targeting the rich and powerful for  their various deceits which range from  funding sketchy nightclubs to faking a  giant drug bust and the most egregious  deceit is also the most widespread the  city's elite have straight up looted the  gotham renewal fund which the wayne  family established to help clean up the  town and by the time we get to the  film's final big reveal that a mafia s  kingpin is puppeteering gotham's entire  government it's clear that these widely  accepted lies go all the way to the top  all this deceit from the city's rich and  powerful made us think of plato's  concept of the noble lie as explored in  a controversial paragraph of his book  the republic he explains that rulers  will need a throng of lies and  deceptions for the benefits of the ruled  basically a noble lie is an untruth that  a society's leaders use to persuade  their citizens to fall into line all in  the name of keeping their society  functioning a common noble lie is that a  given country's land inherently belongs  to its people even if that land was  actually stolen a simple way of thinking  of a noble lie is the way parents might  be dishonest with their child for their  own good plato basically thought that  such lies could be justified if they  served moral ends and means modern  philosophers like leo strauss have  endorsed noble lies as necessary for  social cohesion it's good to keep people  united in their ideology even if that  ideology is falsely constructed and we  see a pretty perverted version of the  noble lie in the batman where a  government filled with apparently good  honest leaders is revealed to be a giant  masquerade these leaders have long  pretended to be genuinely invested in  gotham's renewal when in actuality the  wayne funds were treated as a cash grab  for corruption the not so noble lie that  thomas wayne's funds are being used to  help the city flourish couldn't be  further from the truth gotham's  politicians get to blame the city's  criminal underworld for all the crime  and economic problems while all the  while working alongside those very  criminals to ensure that they're both  making money hand over fist and it's  effective the smoke and mirrors of the  gotham renewal project and the lingering  specter of the criminal underworld have  kept the people distracted while the  select few continue to enjoy their money  and power the lie has arguably fostered  social cohesion although probably not as  effectively as say actually investing in  your city's infrastructure and social  services but hey i digress and  throughout the movie we see more noble  lies but on a smaller scale for example  we learned that batman's dad went to  great lengths to maintain his wife's  reputation eventually this led him to  send a seedy crime boss to intimidate an  investigative reporter who was going to  publish a story about the less savory  details of his wife's personal history  as usually happens when you hire your  local crime boss for a freelance gig but  this resulted in murder all of which is  sounding not super noble in tandem with  this revelation we also see that  batman's vision of his father as the  ultimate pinnacle of human goodness has  too been a noble lie alfred admits that  he didn't tell batman the truth about  his father because he knew little bruce  desperately needed to believe in his  dad's unimpeachable greatness here it's  worth noting a choice that strikes us as  the movie's biggest shortcoming it's  refusal to double down on soluting the  image of thomas wayne see at first  batman thinks that his dad intended to  have the reporter killed a horrifying  prospect that would tarnish thomas's  legacy however he quickly learns that  all thomas wanted to do was scare the  reporter rather than kill him all in the  name of protecting his wife what's more  thomas was racked with guilt in the  aftermath of the murder in fact alfred  even says that thomas was planning to go  to the police and that this may have  been the reason he himself was killed  this felt like a bit of a cop-out and a  missed opportunity to really complicate  the wayne legacy in an interesting way  importantly making thomas wayne a  capital b bad guy would have created  some nice mirroring in the two threads  of the plot we'd have to watch batman  personally reckon with the same thing  the city of gotham is on a larger scale  the fact that the people they admired  most were actually super sus and  blatantly immoral instead it's like  everybody else got santa spoiled for  them while batman just found out that  santa made one really bad choice in the  80s so what happens when noble lies  become intrinsic to a given society's  functioning kind of like your  increasingly elaborate explanation about  why you got home late from school which  had nothing at all to do with the eight  bong ribs at corey's house lies tend to  get more lies a lie then about the  city's biggest drug bust then begets  another lie that the busted drug ring  has been shut down when in actuality  it's continued to flourish by bribing  public officials and as the riddler  reveals the total dishonesty of each  public figure he starts to show how this  cycle of deceit has eaten away at the  foundation of gotham civil society by  the end of the film we learned that just  about every powerful person employed by  the city of gotham has accommodated the  lies and corruption necessary to  maintain their power so obviously the  rich and influential are super duper  guilty although certainly not deserving  of having their faces devoured by rats  but by the end the riddler becomes even  more radical suggesting that all of  gotham is culpable for blindly accepting  the lies fed to them by politicians and  thus are complicit in the creation of  this deceitful society the film itself  reflects some sense of communal  responsibility showing the solemn  beginning of a funeral for the mayor  where presumably nobody was planning to  mention those leaked photos of him with  the young woman who had been battered  but when ridler escalates his attack to  target the entire city of gotham things  start feeling a little religious and not  just because the song ave maria pops up  at least twice in the film see by the  riddler's estimation the sins of the  city's leaders and consequently its  people are too heinous for gotham  society to ever be redeemed that's why  he decides to flood the whole place  ironically by destroying some of the  city's actually functional  infrastructure this can't help but make  us think of the old testament god and  the whole flooding the goddamn world  because people were behaving immorally  thing as if it wasn't harsh enough god  additionally reigned burning sulfur on  the city of sodom specifically for the  reason the riddler floods gotham it was  a corrupt place that failed to use its  resources to take care of its most  vulnerable citizens these evocations are  anything but subtle and don't even get  us started on the whole sins of the  father theme throughout the movie too  but back to the riddler he's a bad guy  for a reason where he sees an  unredeemable society or an unredeemable  family batman sees something else  entirely to understand let's track  batman's personal arc in the film he  starts off really stoked on the idea of  vengeance believing that the best way to  keep people safe is by a giving them  something to fear and b making sure  people are held accountable for their  actions with violence he might not be  drowning an entire city but this  approach is not unlike the riddler's old  testament vibes but throughout the film  this approach to making society a better  place is called into question not the  least of which when the riddler tells  batman that he has been an essential  player in the villain's deranged plot  and even says that they are basically  teammates but the validity of vengeance  as a guiding moral compass is called  into question most explicitly when  batman faces off against the riddler  snipers who have been shooting into a  crowd one of the snipers when asked his  name replies vengeance thus mirroring  batman's ideology back at him obviously  sharing the same mantra as a banana's  group of snipers isn't a good look so  it's no surprise that our hero  ultimately settles on a different view  he decides that rather than needing  someone to fear the people of gotham  really need someone to believe in  someone to inspire hope and as he saves  people from the riddler's giant flood  batman becomes that guy in the process  he also reads kind of like a jesus  figure to the riddler's old testament  god the brave guy willing to make  enormous sacrifices and even risk death  in order to redeem a world filled with  sinners someone who wants justice more  than he does vengeance the answer's  justice but what do you guys think can  noble lies be a justifiable way to keep  any given society humming along would  you rather not know just how deeply  corrupt the rich and powerful are let us  know in the comments big old thanks to  our patrons for all your support and be  sure to check out our podcast punch that  subscribe button like it's a corrupt  civil servant profiteering off a local  drug crisis and don't forget to ring  that bell and as always thanks for  watching  peace  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you

